Type-checking concerns:
- `useComposedRefs(contentRef, handleContentChange)` — contentRef is `React.Ref<ViewportContentMounterElement> | undefined` (since it's optional, so possibly undefined), handleContentChange is `(node: NavigationMenuContentElement | null) => void`. ViewportContentMounterElement = NavigationMenuContentImplElement, and NavigationMenuContentElement = NavigationMenuContentImplElement (line 746). Same type. `useComposedRefs<T>(...refs: PossibleRef<T>[])` → T is inferred from Ref<ViewportContentMounterElement> → T = ViewportContentMounterElement; the callback is `(node: NavigationMenuContentElement | null) => void` — RefCallback<T> = (instance: T | null) => void | cleanup. T = NavigationMenuContentImplElement, and NavigationMenuContentElement is an alias for it. OK.
- The return value of `useComposedRefs` is `React.RefCallback<T>` — assignable to the ref prop of NavigationMenuContentImpl (forwardRef<NavigationMenuContentImplElement, ...>). ✓
- `ScopedProps<NavigationMenuViewportItemProps>` — ScopedProps adds `__scopeNavigationMenu?: Scope`; NavigationMenuContentImplProps extends NavigationMenuContentImplPrivateProps, which presumably contains `__scopeNavigationMenu: Scope`. ✓ (Same pattern as NavigationMenuContent itself on line 758.)
